From 0395ed98581cf9c7c2486caea76eedb0f261b145 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pugant <pugant@localhost>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 15:30:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 05/19] park-hot-RAM-mirror: RAM copy of the park's live entry,
 flag-gated default OFF [w6-8]

W6-8 (A3-5). The T32-b park is persist-only BY DESIGN (bounded RAM); this
inverts that decision ONLY behind the new --cache-disk-park-ram-mirror <MiB>
flag (default 0 = off = today's behavior exactly - both the capture and the
serve paths are gated on park_library && park_ram_mirror_limit > 0).

When on, a park save whose entry payload fits the cap tees the state bytes
into a single-entry RAM mirror right after the tmp file is written and before
fdatasync+DONTNEED drops it (page-cache-hot copy; the read-back CRC is
compared against the stream CRC, so the mirror is verified equal to the
sidecar at birth). The next restore of that entry is served straight from
RAM via llama_state_seq_load_buffer - no disk read, no CRC pass
(read_path=ram-mirror in the load marker, crc_ms stays 0). The mirror dies
with its entry: supersede, budget eviction, boot GC and restore rejection
all erase through erase_disk_state, which drops a matching mirror. A fresh
boot starts mirror-less; an over-cap entry is simply not mirrored. The
restore-time byte counters keep counting the served payload (the mirror is
an internal cache of the library, not a separate source).

Gate N (test-park-mirror, real tiny model, CPU-only): after a mirror save
the entry's state files are DELETED - the ON park still restores with a
payload byte-identical to the ordinary file restore, while the OFF park
fails the identical scenario exactly like today (size-mismatch rejection);
supersede drops the mirror. test-park-library grows the pure-rule coverage
(admission all-or-nothing on the cap, serve matching by entry id + exact
byte counts, draft only when wanted) and the instance-level default-off
gates. User approval for re-enabling park RAM: 10/09, documented in
docs/research/wave6/CARDS-GO.md (W6-8).

Co-Authored-By: GLM by z.ai
